  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:16px">Every archive address above was verified from the
    institution&rsquo;s own published contact page before anything was sent. Where none is published, none
    has been invented.</p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
  <h2>What FamilySearch full text does and does not reach</h2>
  <p class="dek">Recorded 13 September 2026, because it decides which sweeps have to be done by eye.</p>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p>FamilySearch&rsquo;s <strong>full-text search</strong> reads handwriting across images that were
      never name-indexed. It is the difference between a search and an afternoon of reading &mdash; so
      what it covers is a research fact in its own right.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Santa Fe province is not in the corpus.</strong> That is why the Rosario baptism index
        had to be read by eye, eighty openings of it.</li>
      <li><strong>Montevideo is in it</strong> &mdash; parish baptism, marriage, burial and marriage-
        dispensation registers, the Catedral, San Francisco de Asís, the Cordón, Pocitos.</li>
      <li><strong>Buenos Aires City is in it</strong> &mdash; parish registers, probate estate case files,
        even ship-inspection files.</li>
      <li>It found the 1886 Montevideo marriage of the girl from Rosario folio 229 in seconds. It has
        never found Juan Carlos Lerena.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="cap">A null from full text is weaker than a null from reading: the handwriting recognition
      is good, not perfect, and a name it misreads is a name it cannot return. Every negative from it is
      recorded as what it is.</p>
  </div>
</section>


</Base>
